Logic Runtime Mutation
Runtime mutation allows a comp [logic] instance to modify its dynamic fact store.
Mutation is intentionally separate from the logic definition:
• inline [logic] defines facts, rules, and queries.
• comp [logic] owns the runtime dynamic state.
• + adds a dynamic fact.
• - removes a dynamic fact.
• Every mutation block is atomic.
• mutationFailed reports whether the transaction failed.
Runtime mutation does not modify the original inline [logic] definition.

───

Quick reference

Syntax
Meaning

+ fact(...)
Add a dynamic fact

- fact(...)
Remove a dynamic fact

logic { ... }
Mutation body inside an execution block

set = trigger
Starts the execution/transaction

mutationFailed >= wire
1 if the transaction failed, otherwise 0


Example:
logts
.myLogic:{
    logic {
        + allowed("zone2", "container7")
        - allowed("zone1", "container7")
    }

    mutationFailed >= failed
    set = trigger
}


───

Static and Dynamic Facts
Facts defined in inline [logic] are part of the initial knowledge base:
logts
inline [logic] .warehouse:

    container("container1")
    container("container2")

    allowed("zone1", "container1")

:

They describe the initial world.
A comp [logic] instance can additionally maintain dynamic facts.
text
static facts
      +
dynamic facts
      ↓
current logic state

Runtime mutation changes only the dynamic part.
The inline [logic] definition remains unchanged.

───

Mutation Body
Mutations are written inside logic { } in a component execution block:
logts
.myLogic:{
    logic {
        + allowed("zone2", "container1")
        - allowed("zone1", "container1")
    }

    set = trigger
}

logic { } is not an independent execution block.
It is part of the surrounding execution block and is processed when set triggers that block.

───

Add a Fact
Use + to add a dynamic fact:
logts
logic {
    + allowed("zone2", "container1")
}

After a successful commit:
text
allowed(zone2, container1)

is part of the current runtime knowledge.
The fact can subsequently participate in rules and queries.
For example:
logts
canMove(Container) <-
    container(Container),
    allowed("destination", Container)

can see facts added at runtime.

───

Remove a Fact
Use - to remove a dynamic fact:
logts
logic {
    - allowed("zone1", "container1")
}

The fact is removed from the dynamic fact store if the mutation succeeds.
Removing a fact does not modify the rules that may have used that fact.
Derived results are simply recalculated by subsequent queries against the new state.

───

LogTScript Values and Literals
Mutation arguments may contain values coming from LogTScript or explicit literals.
External values
logts
logic {
    + allowed(myDestNameWire, myContainerNameWire)
}

The LogTScript values are evaluated when the execution block runs.
For example, if:
text
myDestNameWire = "zone2"
myContainerNameWire = "container7"

the mutation becomes equivalent to:
logts
+ allowed("zone2", "container7")

Text literals
Text literals use double quotes:
logts
+ allowed("zone2", "container7")

This explicitly represents the text values zone2 and container7.
Numeric literals
Numbers can be used directly:
logts
+ age("john", 25)
+ capacity("container1", 100)

The distinction is:
logts
+ location(boxName, destination)

uses LogTScript values, while:
logts
+ location("box42", "zone3")

uses explicit text literals.

───

Atomic Mutation
All mutations in one execution block form a single transaction.
For example:
logts
.myLogic:{
    logic {
        - inside(box1, conveyor1)
        + inside(box1, container2)
    }

    set = trigger
}

The transaction has two operations:
text
1. remove inside(box1, conveyor1)
2. add    inside(box1, container2)

Both operations must succeed.
If they do:
text
COMMIT

The new state becomes visible.
If any operation fails:
text
ROLLBACK

and none of the mutations are committed.

───

Example — Failed Transaction
Suppose the current state contains:
text
inside(box1, conveyor1)

but container999 is not a valid destination.
The following transaction is attempted:
logts
logic {
    - inside(box1, conveyor1)
    + inside(box1, container999)
}

If the second mutation fails:
text
transaction → FAILED

The first mutation is rolled back as well.
The final state remains:
text
inside(box1, conveyor1)

There is never an intermediate state where box1 has no location.

───

mutationFailed
mutationFailed is a runtime pout of comp [logic].
It is a single-bit value:
text
0 → last mutation transaction succeeded
1 → last mutation transaction failed

It can be redirected like other component pouts:
logts
.myLogic:{
    logic {
        - inside(box1, conveyor1)
        + inside(box1, container2)
    }

    mutationFailed >= failed
    set = trigger
}

With:
logts
1wire failed = 0

a successful transaction produces:
text
failed = 0

A failed transaction produces:
text
failed = 1

The transaction is rolled back when mutationFailed = 1.

───

Mutation Failure vs Query Failure
These are different concepts.
A query can fail to find a solution:
logts
query isAllowed:
    allowed("destination", X)

This is a normal query result:
text
0

It does not mean that the logic component has entered an error state.
A mutation failure is different:
text
mutation
   ↓
cannot be applied
   ↓
ROLLBACK
   ↓
mutationFailed = 1

Mutation failure therefore describes the state transition, not the truth value of a query.

───

Duplicate Adds
Dynamic facts behave as a set.
Adding an already-existing dynamic fact does not create duplicates:
logts
logic {
    + allowed("zone1", "container1")
    + allowed("zone1", "container1")
}

The resulting state contains one fact:
text
allowed(zone1, container1)

The operation does not create multiple copies of the same fact.

───

Removing Facts
A removal operates on matching dynamic facts.
For example:
logts
logic {
    - allowed("zone1", "container1")
}

removes the corresponding dynamic fact.
Mutation failure is reserved for an operation that cannot be legally performed according to the runtime logic/state rules.
This distinction allows the logic engine to validate mutations rather than treating every absent fact as an error.

───

Facts vs Rules
Runtime mutation changes facts, not rules.
Allowed:
logts
logic {
    + container("container3")
    - container("container2")
}

Not allowed:
text
+ rule(...)
- rule(...)

or runtime replacement of a relation definition.
Rules remain part of the inline [logic] definition:
logts
inline [logic] .warehouse:

    canMove(Object, Container) <-
        object(Object),
        container(Container),
        allowed("destination", Container)

:

The runtime state can change:
logts
logic {
    + allowed("destination", "container3")
}

without changing the canMove rule itself.
This gives a clean separation:
text
RULES = program / knowledge model
FACTS = runtime data


───

Derived Knowledge
Rules are evaluated against the current fact state.
Suppose:
logts
inline [logic] .world:

    object("box1")
    container("c1")

    canMove(Object, Container) <-
        object(Object),
        container(Container),
        allowed("destination", Container)

:

Initially:
text
allowed(destination, c1)

does not exist.
Runtime mutation:
logts
logic {
    + allowed("destination", "c1")
}

After the transaction commits, the rule can derive:
text
canMove(box1, c1)

If the fact is later removed:
logts
logic {
    - allowed("destination", "c1")
}

the next query no longer derives that result.
The derived relation does not need to be manually updated.

───

Move as a Transaction
Runtime mutation provides a natural foundation for object movement.
A move can conceptually be represented as:
logts
logic {
    - inside(box1, conveyor1)
    + inside(box1, container2)
}

Because the mutation is atomic, the object cannot be left in an intermediate state if the destination operation fails.
This also makes mutation useful for:
• conveyors;
• containers;
• robots;
• scanners;
• elevators;
• sorting systems;
• warehouse simulations;
• world-state tracking.

───

Runtime State Model
A comp [logic] instance can therefore be viewed as:
text
inline [logic]
       │
       ├── static facts
       ├── rules
       └── queries
       │
       ▼
┌─────────────────────┐
│   comp [logic]      │
│                     │
│ static knowledge    │
│        +            │
│ dynamic fact store  │
│                     │
│ + / - mutations     │
│        │            │
│        ▼            │
│ transactional state │
└──────────┬──────────┘
           │
           ▼
        queries
           │
           ▼
      LogTScript wires

The important rule is:
+ and - modify the runtime world, not the logic definition. Every mutation block is atomic: either all mutations commit, or none of them do. mutationFailed reports the result as a 1-bit runtime pout.



Logic Constraints
Constraints define conditions that must remain true when the runtime state is modified.
Unlike normal relations, constraints are not used to produce alternative solutions. They are validation rules for runtime mutations performed by comp [logic].
A mutation transaction is committed only when all applicable constraints succeed.

───

Quick reference

Syntax
Meaning

constraint name(X, Y) <- ...
Defines a validation constraint

+ fact(...)
Proposes adding a fact

- fact(...)
Proposes removing a fact

Multiple constraints with same predicate
All must succeed

Constraint failure
Transaction is rolled back

mutationFailed
1 when the transaction fails



───

Basic constraint
Consider a world containing objects and containers:
logts
inline [logic] .warehouse:

    object("box1")
    container("c1")

    constraint inside(Object, Container) <-
        object(Object),
        container(Container)

:

This says that an inside(Object, Container) fact is valid only when:
• Object is an existing object;
• Container is an existing container.
A component can then attempt:
logts
.logic:{
    logic {
        + inside("box1", "c1")
    }

    set = trigger
}

The proposed state is validated against the constraint.
If it succeeds:
text
COMMIT
mutationFailed = 0


───

Constraint failure
Suppose the component attempts:
logts
logic {
    + inside("box1", "unknownContainer")
}

The proposed fact does not satisfy:
logts
container("unknownContainer")

Therefore:
text
CONSTRAINT FAILED
      ↓
ROLLBACK
      ↓
mutationFailed = 1

The fact is not added.

───

Constraints validate the resulting state
Constraints are evaluated against the temporary state produced by the complete transaction, not independently against the old state.
For example:
logts
logic {
    - inside("box1", "c1")
    + inside("box1", "c2")
}

The engine conceptually performs:
text
current state
      ↓
temporary state
      ↓
remove inside(box1,c1)
      ↓
add inside(box1,c2)
      ↓
validate constraints
      ↓
COMMIT

This is important for constraints such as uniqueness or capacity.

───

Multiple constraints
Multiple constraints may use the same predicate and arity.
logts
constraint inside(Object, Container) <-
    object(Object),
    container(Container)

constraint inside(Object, Container) <-
    allowed(Object, Container)

constraint inside(Object, Container) <-
    capacityAvailable(Container)

These constraints are cumulative.
For:
logts
+ inside("box1", "c1")

all applicable constraints must succeed:
text
inside(box1,c1)
    │
    ├── constraint #1 ✓
    ├── constraint #2 ✓
    └── constraint #3 ✓
              │
              ▼
            COMMIT

If any constraint fails:
text
constraint #2 ✗
      ↓
ROLLBACK
      ↓
mutationFailed = 1


───

Constraints vs Relations
Normal relations and constraints have different semantics.
Relation
Multiple clauses are alternatives:
logts
canMove(X, Y) <-
    vehicle(X),
    road(Y)

canMove(X, Y) <-
    robot(X),
    corridor(Y)

The clauses represent alternative ways to prove canMove/2.
Constraint
Multiple declarations are cumulative:
logts
constraint inside(X, Y) <-
    object(X)

constraint inside(X, Y) <-
    container(Y)

constraint inside(X, Y) <-
    allowed(X, Y)

All applicable constraints must succeed.
Therefore:
text
relation
    same predicate/arity → alternatives (OR)

constraint
    same predicate/arity → cumulative validation (AND)


───

Example — Container Capacity
A useful example is container capacity.
logts
inline [logic] .warehouse:

    object("box1")
    object("box2")

    container("c1")

    capacity("c1", 2)

    constraint inside(Object, Container) <-
        object(Object),
        container(Container),
        capacityAvailable(Container)

:

The exact implementation of capacityAvailable/1 can use the current runtime facts.
The important part is that the constraint is evaluated against the proposed state.
If the container is already full:
logts
logic {
    + inside("box3", "c1")
}

the constraint fails:
text
mutationFailed = 1

and the mutation is discarded.

───

Example — One Object, One Location
A useful invariant is that an object cannot be inside two containers simultaneously.
logts
constraint inside(Object, Container) <-
    object(Object),
    container(Container),
    singleLocation(Object)

A transaction such as:
logts
logic {
    + inside("box1", "c2")
}

must be evaluated against the complete resulting state.
If the old state contains:
text
inside(box1, c1)

and the transaction only adds:
text
inside(box1, c2)

the constraint can reject the resulting state.
Instead, moving the object can be represented atomically:
logts
logic {
    - inside("box1", "c1")
    + inside("box1", "c2")
}

The resulting state contains only the new location, so the transaction can succeed.

───

Constraints and Runtime Mutation
Constraints do not modify the world themselves.
They only decide whether a proposed mutation is valid.
text
+ fact
                │
                ▼
        temporary state
                │
                ▼
          constraints
                │
        ┌───────┴───────┐
        │               │
      valid           invalid
        │               │
        ▼               ▼
     COMMIT          ROLLBACK
        │               │
        ▼               ▼
mutationFailed=0   mutationFailed=1

This keeps the responsibilities separate:
text
Facts       → runtime state
Relations   → derive knowledge
Queries     → ask questions
Constraints → validate state transitions
+ / -       → request state changes


───

Important property — atomicity
A transaction containing several mutations is never partially committed.
logts
logic {
    - inside("box1", "c1")
    + inside("box1", "c2")
    + inside("box1", "invalid")
}

If the final addition violates a constraint:
text
transaction
              │
      ┌───────┴────────┐
      │                │
    remove            add
      ✓                ✓
      │                │
      └───────┬────────┘
              │
       invalid addition
              ✗
              │
          ROLLBACK
              │
              ▼
      original state

The successful operations are rolled back as well.
mutationFailed becomes 1.

───

Summary
Constraints provide a clean way to turn the logic engine into a state-validating world model:
text
inline [logic]
    facts
    relations
    queries
    constraints
          │
          ▼
comp [logic]
    + / - mutations
          │
          ▼
    atomic transaction
          │
          ▼
    validate constraints
       /          \
   valid          invalid
    │                │
 COMMIT           ROLLBACK
    │                │
    0                1
mutationFailed  mutationFailed

The key rule is:
Relations explain what can be derived. Constraints define which runtime states are allowed. Mutations change the state only when the complete resulting state satisfies all applicable constraints.

